= Liquidity pool =

== Overview ==
'''Liquidity pool''' is a set of assets committed under shared rules so that participants or protocols can draw on those assets for trading, lending, settlement, or other liquidity-demanding operations.

In an AMM, the pool commonly holds reserves against which traders execute swaps. Liquidity providers contribute assets and receive some claim on fees, reserves, or protocol accounting depending on the design.

A pool is not itself an automated market maker: the AMM is the pricing/execution mechanism, while the pool is the inventory or shared asset state on which that mechanism operates.

Pool risk can include smart-contract failure, asset/counterparty risk, adverse selection, price divergence, oracle dependencies, liquidity concentration, and protocol/governance risk.

== Boundaries ==
* liquidity pool ≠ AMM
* pool share ≠ guaranteed principal value
* pooled liquidity ≠ unlimited market depth

